.TH ESDM\-CDEV\-CHECK 8 "2026-10-20" "ESDM @VERSION@" "ESDM Manual"
.SH NAME
esdm\-cdev\-check \- check the esdm_cdev kernel module against the ESDM
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B esdm\-cdev\-check
.I check
.RI [ args ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B esdm\-cdev\-check
runs the checks of the
.I esdm_cdev
kernel module, which serves
.IR /dev/esdm_random ,
.I /dev/esdm_urandom
and
.I /proc/esdm
from the ESDM over its EGD socket (see
.I kernel/esdm_cdev/README.md
of the source tree). Each check is a subcommand: it prints what it found and
exits with 0 when that is what the module promises, and with a line starting
with
.B FAIL:
and 1 when it is not.
.PP
The checks talk to the loaded module and to the ESDM serving it; they need
neither an ESDM of their own nor a build with test hooks. Most of them expect
the ESDM to be running and reachable over its EGD socket
.RB ( esdm\-server\-egd.socket ,
or
.B esdm\-server \-\-egd_socket
.IR @EGD_SOCKET_PATH@ ).
Some change the state of that ESDM or of the module, and are meant for a
machine set up for them \- the NixOS VM test of the ESDM flake runs every check
with the ESDM running, stopped and restarted, and against the fake EGD server
of
.BR fake\-egd .
.PP
The program is built with the test suite, whose
.B esdm_cdev
suite runs the checks that hold next to the ESDM of an ordinary machine. It is
installed with the meson option
.BR esdm\-cdev\-check ,
for a machine without a build tree. It is a test program and has no place in a
production deployment.
.SH CHECKS
.SS The devices
.TP
.BR reads " [" sync ]
Reads of 1 byte to 1 MiB from both devices are random, no two reads are equal,
and single bytes take more than a few values. With
.BR sync ,
the reads are opened with
.B O_SYNC
and served by the prediction resistance DRNG, up to 64 bytes each.
.TP
.B nonblock
A read with
.B O_NONBLOCK
of an operational ESDM gets random data.
.TP
.B poll
.I /dev/esdm_random
is readable once the ESDM is operational;
.I /dev/esdm_urandom
has no
.BR poll ()
and is readable and writable as any file without one.
.TP
.B writes
Writes of nothing up to 300 KiB are taken in full, each in one
.BR write ().
.SS The ioctls
.TP
.B ioctl\-priv
Needs CAP_SYS_ADMIN. Every ioctl of
.BR random (4)
does to the pool of the ESDM what it does to the kernel's, and the arguments
the kernel refuses are refused. It credits entropy nobody delivered and clears
the pool of the ESDM.
.TP
.B ioctl\-unpriv
Without CAP_SYS_ADMIN, the entropy count can be read and nothing else is done,
and the writable files of
.I /proc/esdm
are refused. A caller with the capability gives it up first.
.SS /proc/esdm
.TP
.B proc
Needs CAP_SYS_ADMIN. The files of
.I /proc/esdm
with the ESDM reachable: their content, the two that can be written, and what
is refused there. It sets
.B write_wakeup_threshold
and
.B urandom_min_reseed_secs
of the ESDM, and restores them.
.SS The ESDM unreachable
.TP
.B unreachable
With the ESDM not reachable, nothing is served from elsewhere:
.I /dev/esdm_urandom
fails with ENOTCONN,
.I /dev/esdm_random
waits \- with
.BR O_NONBLOCK ,
EAGAIN \- and is not readable for
.BR poll ().
Writes, the ioctls and the files of
.I /proc/esdm
fail with ENOTCONN.
.SS Readers in the background, and speed
.TP
.BI loop " dev seconds"
Reads of 4096 bytes from
.I dev
for that long, which get random data and no error across a restart of the
ESDM. Ends with the line
.BR "loop reads=... errors=..." .
.TP
.BI block " dev"
A reader that stays in
.BR read ()
until it is killed, or reads on when a read returns. It prints
.B blocking
once the device is open; from then on, the module cannot be unloaded.
.TP
.BR throughput " [\fIsocket\fR]"
Informational: the speed of reads of 32 bytes, of one EGD transfer, of a page
and of 64 KiB through each device, and through the EGD socket of the ESDM from
user space for comparison \- by default
.IR @EGD_SOCKET_PATH@ .
.TP
.B stalled\-writers
Writers whose data never comes \- their buffer lies in a range of a
.BR userfaultfd (2)
nobody serves \- more of them than the module has connections, must not keep
anybody else from the ESDM. A userfaultfd catching the faults of the kernel
needs CAP_SYS_PTRACE or
.BR vm.unprivileged_userfaultfd=1 .
.SS Against a fake EGD server
.TP
.BI fake\-egd " socket log " notop | mute
An EGD server on
.I socket
in place of the ESDM, for what the real one cannot be made to do: answer like an
ESDM that is not operational yet
.RB ( notop ),
or accept connections and never answer
.RB ( mute ).
It writes
.B listening
to
.I log
once it listens, and every connection and every command that changes something
after that. The module is pointed at it with its parameter
.BR egd_socket .
.TP
.BI addentropy " log"
Needs CAP_SYS_ADMIN; against a
.B notop
fake EGD server.
.B RNDADDENTROPY
inserts all of the data, which carries no entropy claim, and credits it after
that in one go, with no more than the data has bits.
.TP
.BI addtoentcnt\-signal " log"
Needs CAP_SYS_ADMIN; against a
.B notop
fake EGD server, which answers the crediting a second late. A signal while the
module waits for the answer must not have the ioctl restarted, which would
carry out the command once more.
.TP
.B poll\-notop
Against a
.B notop
fake EGD server:
.BR poll ()
of
.I /dev/esdm_random
reports it readable only when a read with
.B O_NONBLOCK
gets data \- that is, not at all.
.TP
.BI backoff " log seconds"
Against a
.B mute
fake EGD server: the reads fail, with ETIMEDOUT once a connection is made, and
with ENOTCONN while the module waits before it makes the next, up to 2
seconds.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The check passed.
.TP
.B 1
The check failed, as the
.B FAIL:
line says.
.TP
.B 2
Unknown check or wrong arguments: the usage, with every check, is printed.
.TP
.B 77
The check was skipped, as the line starting with
.B SKIP:
says: the module is not loaded, or the check needs CAP_SYS_ADMIN, as root of the
initial user namespace, and does not have it. 77 is what
.B meson test
takes for a skipped test.
.SH EXAMPLES
The checks of the meson suite, next to a running ESDM serving its EGD socket:
.PP
.RS
.nf
modprobe esdm_cdev
esdm\-cdev\-check reads
esdm\-cdev\-check reads sync
esdm\-cdev\-check nonblock
esdm\-cdev\-check poll
esdm\-cdev\-check writes
esdm\-cdev\-check ioctl\-unpriv
esdm\-cdev\-check proc
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR esdm\-server (8),
.BR esdm\-cuse\-random (8),
.BR esdm\-proc (8),
.BR libesdm_egd_client (3),
.BR random (4)
.SH AUTHOR
Markus Theil <theil.markus@gmail.com>
